    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born around 1840 into a farming family at Besease, Yaa Asantewaa became Queen Mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ejisu">Ejisu</a> through her brother, the Ejisuhene, an office that carried responsibility for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> and a voice in choosing the next king.</li><li>When the British exiled King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> in 1896 and their governor demanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, she urged the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> leaders to fight, and in 1900 she was chosen war leader, the first and only woman given that role in Asante history.</li><li>The rebellion of 1900 besieged the British fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> and ended in defeat and her exile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, where she died in 1921; the Stool itself was never handed over, and she is remembered in Ghana as a figure of resistance to colonial rule.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the council that debated what to do about the governor's demand for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, Yaa Asantewaa is said to have told the chiefs to exchange their loincloths for her undergarments if they would not fight, and then to have seized a gun and fired a shot in front of the men.</p></aside>
    </section>

