    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1840</span><span class="rf-text">Yaa Asantewaa is born in Besease, daughter of Kwaku Ampoma and Ata Po</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1883</span><span class="rf-text">A civil war begins in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> Confederacy and lasts until 1888</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1894</span><span class="rf-text">Her brother dies, and as Queen Mother she nominates her own grandson as Ejisuhene</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">The British exile her grandson, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> leaders to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>; she becomes regent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ejisu">Ejisu</a>–Juaben district</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> lay siege to the British fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>; she is chosen war leader of the Asante forces</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1902</span><span class="rf-text">The British annex the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante Empire</a> and make the Asante a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1921</span><span class="rf-text">She dies in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> and the other exiles are allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a>, and her remains are returned for a royal burial</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Yaa Asantewaa was born around 1840 in Besease, the daughter of Kwaku Ampoma and Ata Po. Her brother Afrane Panin became the chief of Edweso, a nearby community. Her childhood passed without incident, and she grew up to cultivate crops on the land around Boankra. She entered a polygamous marriage with a man from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> and had a daughter. She was a successful farmer and mother.</p><p>She belonged to a society organised on matrilineal lines, in which lineage is traced through women descended from a common female ancestor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> held that a person's blood comes from the mother and spirit from the father. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a>, the hierarchy of male stools was complemented by female counterparts. The head of a division, the ohene, and the head of an autonomous political community, the Amanhene, each had a female counterpart, the ohemaa, who sat on their councils. The ohemaa and the ohene shared the same mogya, or localised matrilineage. In a village, the heads of the matrilineages formed the village council, and for each village an obaa panyin looked after the affairs of its women and sat on that council. Women who held stools took part in judicial and legislative business, in the making and unmaking of war, and in the distribution of land.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-stool.jpg" alt="Golden Stool" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_stool_31_January_1935.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UK gov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Her older brother, Nana Akwasi Afrane Okpase, held a powerful position in the empire and selected her as Queen Mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ejisu">Ejisu</a>. The post was a prestigious one. The Queen Mother was responsible for protecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, advising the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> and choosing candidates for the next king. The queen mother was the sister of the chief and head of kinship relations.</p><p>During her brother's time in office, she saw the Ashanti Confederacy pass through events that threatened its future, among them a civil war from 1883 to 1888. Her brother died in 1894, and she used her right as Queen Mother to nominate her own grandson as Ejisuhene. In 1896 the British exiled this grandson to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, along with the King of Asante, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a>, and other members of the Asante government. Yaa Asantewaa then became regent of the Ejisu–Juaben district. After Prempeh's exile, the British governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, Frederick Hodgson, demanded the Golden Stool, the symbol of the Asante nation. The demand led the remaining members of the Asante government to meet secretly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> to discuss how to secure their king's return.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The members of the council disagreed on how to proceed. Yaa Asantewaa, who was present, rose and addressed them. She asked how a proud and brave people could sit back while white men took away their king and chiefs and humiliated them with a demand for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, which she said meant only money to the white men. She said she would not pay one predwan to the governor, and that if the chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> behaved like cowards and would not fight, they should exchange their loincloths for her undergarments. To show her determination she seized a gun and fired a shot in front of the men.</p><p>A number of regional Asante kings chose her as war leader of the Asante fighting force, the first and only time in Asante history that a woman was given that role. A longer speech is also attributed to her, in which she recalled that the British had occupied the land and declared Ashanti a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, had burnt the palace in the reign of King Karikari, and had arrested and deported the king, and she called on the men to defend the Golden Stool. She challenged gender roles and urged women to stand up to fight. The war, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-golden-stool">War of the Golden Stool</a> or the Yaa Asantewaa War, was fought with an army of 5,000 under her leadership. From March 1900 the rebels laid siege to the British fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, where the British had sought refuge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Her chief opponents were the British colonial authorities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> governor, Frederick Hodgson, demanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, which had been a revered symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> nation's soul since the 17th century. After several months of siege, the governor sent a force of 1,400 to put down the rebellion. More than 2,000 Ashanti warriors lost their lives in the fighting, and approximately 1,000 British troops were also killed. This was the highest death toll of the Anglo-Asante wars. The fighting lasted six months. Yaa Asantewaa and a group of her advisers were captured and sent into exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>.</p><p>There was disagreement on the Asante side as well. When the remaining leaders met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> to decide how to win back their king, they disagreed on the course to take, and her speech was aimed at those who hesitated. She questioned the male leaders' response to British colonisation. Despite the British victory, the Golden Stool was never turned over: a false one was delivered to the governor and the real Stool was kept hidden.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prempeh-i.jpg" alt="Prempeh I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prempeh_of_Ashanti_and_followers_(Political_Prisoners)_Mah%C3%A9,_Seychelles.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The rebellion was the last of the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> wars, a series that ran through the 19th century. On 1 January 1902 the British annexed the territory that the Asante Empire had controlled for almost a century, and the Asante became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the British crown. Yaa Asantewaa and her advisers had by then been sent into exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> of her closest advisers went with her.</p><p>She died in exile in the Seychelles on 17 October 1921. Three years after her death, on 17 December 1924, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> and the other remaining members of the exiled Asante court were allowed to return to Asante. Prempeh made sure that the remains of Yaa Asantewaa and the other exiled Ashantis were returned for a proper royal burial. In 1920 a group of African railroad builders found the hidden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> and vandalised it. The Ashanti people sentenced them to death, but the British authorities exiled them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> colony before they were killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> gained independence as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> on 6 March 1957, which is presented as the realisation of her dream of an Asante free of colonial rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a> is still used to initiate and crown the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>. She remains a known figure in Ashanti history and in the broader history of Ghana for her part in confronting British colonialism, and Ghanaians today see her as a queen mother who exercised her political and social right to defend her kingdom. A song remembers her as the woman who fights before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>.</p><p>The Yaa Asantewaa Girls' Secondary School was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> in 1960. In 2000, the hundredth anniversary of the war, Ghana held a week-long celebration with a festival, an international conference, a women's convention and a funeral service for her remains; a museum was dedicated to her at Kwaso in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ejisu">Ejisu</a>–Juaben District on 3 August 2000. A fire on 23 July 2004 destroyed several items there, among them her sandals and battle dress. The current queen mother of Ejisu is Yaa Asantewaa II. The Yaa Asantewaa Centre, an African–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> arts and community centre, was established in Maida Vale, west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1986 and named after her.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Yaa Asantewaa (c. 1840–1921) was Queen Mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ejisu">Ejisu</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>. After the British exiled King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> and demanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, she was chosen war leader of the Asante in 1900. The war ended in defeat and her exile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, where she died.</em></p>

